<!--
  StartupResources config — values applied at new-game start.

  Attributes:
    gold        = denars granted to each alive Lord hero of this culture at new-game start
                  (player clan is skipped; see playerGold for the player). Default 0.
    influence   = influence granted to each eligible CLAN of this culture at new-game start
                  (StartupInfluenceService applies to clans, not lords). Default 0.
    playerGold  = denars granted to the PLAYER hero at CC finalize, by selected culture.
                  Range [0, 10_000_000]. Out-of-range or non-numeric values revert to 0
                  with a logged warning. Default 0 (no warning when missing).

  Edits to this file take effect on the next Bannerlord process restart, not save reload.

  ====================== THIS SUPERSEDES THE 2026-08-14 FLATTENING ======================
  Earlier the same day, commit 4f72e160 flattened this file to 250,000 gold and 1,000
  influence for every culture (elves 500,000) so that "no faction opens the campaign with a
  structural economic head start". That is not what flat denars buy. A culture's troops cost
  between 6.91 and 21.66 denars a day per head, measured (see below), so an identical 250,000
  funds 1.93x more campaign for Mordor than for Gundabad, and an identical 500,000 buys
  Rivendell less than Lothlorien. Flat in denars is not flat in effect.

  If you are reading the flattening's header text somewhere, it is history, not policy.
  Do not restore it without re-reading the wage measurement first.

  ============================ HOW THE gold NUMBERS ARE DERIVED ============================
  `gold` is paid PER LORD, so a culture's real treasury is the value below times its lord
  count, and lord counts run from 10 (Umbar, Lindon) to 150 (Moria). One rule sets them:

      gold = K x runwayDays x avgTroopWage        K = 100.0

  avgTroopWage is measured, not chosen, and it models what the engine actually charges:

    - Each noble clan's roster is its own `default_party_template` from characters/clans.xml
      and spclans.xslt. 176 of the 193 lord-party templates are per CLAN, not per culture, so
      reading the culture defaults would measure rosters almost nobody fields. Khand
      (battania) is the one culture whose clans bind nothing; spcultures.xslt falls it through
      to `kingdom_hero_party_rhun_template`, so it carries Rhûn's cost, not a Variag one.
    - Each stack is weighted by its EXPECTED spawn count, (min + max) / 2, per
      docs/reference/party-template-sizing.md.
    - Each troop's wage is what TroopCostService.GetCharacterWage would return: the T0-T10
      table, tier = clamp(ceil((level - 5) / 5), 0, 10), times 1.3 if the troop is mounted,
      then TRUNCATED to int exactly as the engine truncates. A mounted T5 costs 15, not 15.6.
      Mounted is read from the troop's `default_group` (the four Cavalry/HorseArcher groups),
      which is what CharacterObject.IsMounted resolves to for a non-hero.
    - Party-wage feats are folded in: mordor x1.20, gundabad x1.10, umbar x1.08, and Rohan's
      taom_rohan_mounted_wage -15% on its mounted share (the only mounted WAGE feat in TAOM).
      Garrison-wage feats are NOT folded in: they are settlement-scoped and never touch
      field-party burn.
    - The mercenary x1.5 multiplier is not modelled because no mercenary-occupation troop
      appears in any hero party template. The derivation asserts this rather than assuming it.

  What it still does not model, and cannot: vanilla's per-hero perk and policy wage modifiers
  (DeepPockets, Frugal, MilitaryCoronae, EfficientCampaigner and the rest) all land on the same
  ExplainedNumber at runtime and depend on which lord holds which perk in a given campaign.
  This is a design-time proxy for field-party burn, not the runtime figure.

  runwayDays is the only judgement call, four tiers:
      A = 270 (mythic treasuries)   B = 150 (great realms)
      C = 100 (regional powers)     D =  70 (marginal realms)

  K is the assumed sustained party size, and that is the whole reason it moved on 2026-08-18.
  Rearranged, the real runway a lord gets is K x runwayDays / N for a party of N men, so the
  tiers above only mean what they say when N equals K. K was 52.5437, which is roughly the
  50-150 band a vanilla PartySizeLimit actually allowed, so the arithmetic held.

  NOTE 2026-09-01: the AI Party Size knobs now ship NEUTRAL (multiplier 1.0, flat 0, relief 0), so
  the derivation below describes an opt-in state, not the shipped one. K = 100 was left alone rather
  than re-derived; it sits inside the vanilla 40-203 band but is probably still biased high, because
  the original K = 52.5437 was population-weighted across every lord template and low-tier lords far
  outnumber tier-4 clan leaders. A proper re-derivation is owed.

  Issue #461 can raise AI lords toward ~1,000 men and waive 90% of their wage bill, so the burn
  the runway has to fund is N x (1 - relief) = 1000 x 0.10 = 100. Hence K = 100. Every gold
  value scaled by 100 / 52.5437 = 1.9032 and re-rounded; the world pool moved from about
  2.16M to 4.10M per-lord denars. If you retune AI Wage Relief or the party-size knobs in MCM,
  this K is what goes stale: recompute it as targetPartySize x (1 - wageRelief).

  Values round to the nearest 5,000. Three knobs move everything now: the tier a culture sits
  in, K, and the wage relief K is derived against. Re-derive with the measurement above rather
  than nudging a single row, or the rate stops meaning anything.

  Cultures not listed here receive nothing, silently. Goblin-town and Moria shipped that way
  for two months. StartupResourcesConfigCoverageTests fails if a lord-owning culture is
  missing a row, so do not add a culture to characters/lords.xml without adding it here.

  WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX: fief income per lord still spans 25x in units of "days of one party's
  wages funded per lord". Dol Guldur fields 127 lords on 23 fiefs and Umbar 10 on 40. Startup
  gold only delays that; the fix is roster size. See docs/features/startup-resources.md.
  =========================================================================================
-->
<StartupResources>
  <!-- Elven cultures. Their troops cost roughly three times per head what Mordor's do, and
       their rosters are 30-40% mounted, so an elven denar buys far less campaign than a human
       one; the high rates here are that cost, not a bonus. -->
  <Culture id="rivendell"   gold="580000" influence="600"  playerGold="4000"  />
  <Culture id="lothlorien"  gold="440000" influence="600"  playerGold="4000"  />
  <Culture id="mirkwood"    gold="515000" influence="600"  playerGold="4000"  />
  <Culture id="lindon"      gold="475000" influence="600"  playerGold="4000"  />

  <!-- Dwarven cultures. Erebor sat at 800,000 before the flattening, by a wide margin the
       richest culture on the map; it fields no cavalry at all, so it was also paying a mounted
       premium it never incurs. -->
  <Culture id="erebor"      gold="275000" influence="600"  playerGold="3500"  />

  <!-- Human Good cultures -->
  <Culture id="gondor"      gold="150000"  influence="600"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="vlandia"     gold="75000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="sturgia"     gold="85000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />

  <!-- Human Evil / Eastern cultures. Rhûn's rate is the highest of this group because its
       clans field the costliest human rosters on the map (13.03/head), not as a favour. -->
  <Culture id="empire"      gold="85000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="battania"    gold="105000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="aserai"      gold="85000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="khuzait"     gold="200000" influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />

  <!-- Independent Harad-region kingdoms (full kingdoms with NPC clans + lords; Shaghana has 9
       clans and 90 lords, Abanissa 8 clans and 80 lords) -->
  <!-- Both cultures are now fully playable in character creation (the issue-#111 narrative-menu
       coverage has landed), so their `playerGold` rows below are live. -->
  <Culture id="shaghana"    gold="85000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="abanissa"    gold="85000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />

  <!-- Orcish / Dark cultures. Mordor, Gundabad and Umbar each carry a party-wage PENALTY feat
       (+20%, +10%, +8%) that is already folded into these rates. Mordor still lands lowest of
       the group because its rosters are the cheapest per head on the map even after the +20%. -->
  <Culture id="mordor"      gold="105000"  influence="600"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="isengard"    gold="75000"  influence="600"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="gundabad"    gold="135000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="dolguldur"   gold="115000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="umbar"       gold="160000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />

  <!-- Goblin-town and the Moria orcs. Both shipped CC-selectable in June 2026 with no row here at
       all, so every player who picked one started on 0 denars — and because a missing culture is
       documented above as a silent default of 0, nothing logged it. -->
  <Culture id="goblin"             gold="65000"  influence="200"  playerGold="2000"  />
  <Culture id="mistymountainorcs"  gold="135000"  influence="400"  playerGold="2000"  />

  <!-- Promoted out of a borrowed culture 2026-08-10: Blue Craig off goblin, Lindon off rivendell.
       Each needs its own row: before the split they inherited nothing, because these values are
       keyed on culture and their kingdoms had none of their own. Blue Craig's five clans still
       field goblin rosters (they bind ..._goblin_bluecraig_N_template), which is why its rate
       matches Goblin-town's almost exactly. Lindon sits with the other elven realms above. -->
  <Culture id="bluecraig"          gold="65000"  influence="200"  playerGold="2000"  />
</StartupResources>
